.\" Copyright (c) 2026, LunLink contributors.
.\" It takes the place of the manual page of the original iSCSI Initiator,
.\" (c) 2013-2015 Nareg Sinenian, whose command syntax it describes.
.Dd October 12, 2026
.Dt ISCSICTL 8
.Os macOS
.Sh NAME
.Nm iscsictl
.Nd iSCSI initiator configuration and management tool
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm add target
.Ar target , Ns Ar portal
.Op Fl interface Ar interface
.Nm
.Cm remove target
.Ar target Ns Op , Ns Ar portal
.Pp
.Nm
.Cm login
.Ar target Ns Op , Ns Ar portal
.Nm
.Cm logout
.Ar target
.Op Fl force
.Pp
.Nm
.Cm modify initiator-config
.Op Ar options
.Nm
.Cm modify target-config
.Ar target Ns Op , Ns Ar portal
.Op Ar options
.Nm
.Cm modify discovery-config
.Op Ar options
.Pp
.Nm
.Cm list initiator-config
.Nm
.Cm list target-config
.Ar target
.Nm
.Cm list discovery-config
.Pp
.Nm
.Cm add discovery-portal
.Ar portal
.Op Fl interface Ar interface
.Nm
.Cm remove discovery-portal
.Ar portal
.Nm
.Cm discover
.Ar portal
.Pp
.Nm
.Cm list targets
.Nm
.Cm list luns
.Pp
.Nm
.Cm daemon
.Cm status | start | stop | install | uninstall
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility is used to configure the iSCSI initiator and to manage its sessions.
Targets are defined along with the portals through which they are reached,
either by hand or by discovery.
Logging in to a target attaches its logical units to the system as disks,
whose volumes are then mounted like those of any other disk.
.Pp
All work is carried out by the iSCSI daemon,
.Xr iscsid 8 ,
which
.Nm
starts if it is not running.
Neither the daemon nor
.Nm
require administrator privileges; the configuration and the sessions are
those of the user.
.Pp
A
.Ar target
is named by its iSCSI qualified name (IQN) or EUI-64 identifier.
A
.Ar portal
is an IPv4 address, an IPv6 address or a host name, optionally followed by
a colon and a TCP port (the default port is 3260).
An IPv6 address must be enclosed in brackets if a port is specified.
An
.Ar interface
is the name of a network interface (as listed by
.Xr ifconfig 8 )
from which to connect; by default the system chooses.
.Pp
The commands are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm add target
Defines a target, or adds a portal to a target that is defined already.
.It Cm remove target
Removes a target along with its settings, or just one of its portals.
.It Cm login
Logs in to a target and attaches its logical units as disks.
Unless a portal is specified, the portals of the target are tried one after
another until one of them works.
.It Cm logout
Unmounts the volumes of a target, detaches its disks and logs out.
The command fails if a volume is in use, unless
.Fl force
is specified, in which case data that was not yet written is lost.
.It Cm modify
Changes the configuration of the initiator, of a target or of discovery;
see the options below.
.It Cm list
Displays the configuration of the initiator, of a target or of discovery,
the targets with their state, or the logical units of the targets that are
logged in to along with the names of their disks.
.It Cm add discovery-portal
Adds a portal that is queried periodically for the targets that it offers
(SendTargets discovery).
Targets that are discovered are added automatically and are removed when
they are no longer offered, unless they are logged in to.
.It Cm remove discovery-portal
Removes a discovery portal along with the targets that it contributed.
.It Cm discover
Queries a portal once for the targets that it offers and lists them, without
recording anything.
.It Cm daemon
Displays the state of the daemon, starts or stops it, or installs
.Pq uninstalls
a launch agent that starts the daemon when the user logs in, which is when
targets that are configured for automatic login get connected.
.El
.Pp
The following options can be used to modify initiator-config:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl node-name Ar initiator_IQN
The iSCSI qualified name of the initiator.
Targets may grant access by this name.
By default a name is derived from the identifier of the host.
.It Fl node-alias Ar alias
An alias for the initiator (presented to targets during login).
.It Fl authentication Ar method
The method by which the initiator authenticates itself to targets and
discovery portals: None or CHAP.
.It Fl CHAP-name Ar name
The CHAP user name of the initiator.
.It Fl CHAP-secret
Sets the CHAP secret of the initiator.
The secret is prompted for (or read from the standard input if that is not
a terminal).
.El
.Pp
The following options can be used to modify target-config:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl auto-login Ar enable | disable
Whether the daemon logs in to the target when it starts.
.It Fl persistent Ar enable | disable
Whether the daemon keeps trying to log in to the target again after its
session was lost for good.
.It Fl read-only Ar enable | disable
Whether the disks of the target are attached read-only.
.It Fl HeaderDigest Ar digest
The header digest to ask for: None or CRC32C.
.It Fl DataDigest Ar digest
The data digest to ask for: None or CRC32C.
.It Fl initiator-authentication Ar method
The method by which the initiator authenticates itself to this target: None,
CHAP, or default to use what is configured for the initiator in general.
.It Fl initiator-CHAP-name Ar name
The CHAP user name that the initiator uses for this target.
.It Fl initiator-CHAP-secret
Sets the CHAP secret that the initiator uses for this target.
.It Fl authentication Ar method
The method by which the target authenticates itself to the initiator
(mutual CHAP): None or CHAP.
This requires that the initiator authenticates itself by CHAP as well.
.It Fl CHAP-name Ar name
The CHAP user name by which the target identifies itself.
.It Fl CHAP-secret
Sets the CHAP secret of the target.
.It Fl port Ar port , Fl interface Ar interface
Change the TCP port or the network interface of the specified portal.
.El
.Pp
The following options can be used to modify discovery-config:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl SendTargets Ar enable | disable
Whether discovery portals are queried periodically.
.It Fl interval Ar seconds
The time between queries.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev ISCSI_RUNTIME_DIR
The directory for run-time files (see
.Sx FILES ) .
.It Ev ISCSI_PREFERENCES_DOMAIN
The preferences domain that holds the configuration.
.It Ev ISCSI_SECRETS_FILE
A property list file in which to keep CHAP secrets, instead of the keychain.
.El
.Pp
These variables must have the same values for the daemon and its clients.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/Library/Preferences/io.github.edisonwh6.LunLink.plist
The configuration: the name and alias of the initiator, targets with their
portals and settings, discovery portals.
.It Pa ~/Library/Application Support/LunLink
Run-time files: the socket of the daemon and the mount point of the disk
export.
.It Pa ~/Library/Logs/LunLink/iscsid.log
The log of the daemon.
.El
.Pp
CHAP secrets are kept in the login keychain, as generic passwords of the
service
.Dq LunLink .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Find out which targets a server offers, define one of them and log in:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
iscsictl discover 192.168.1.100
iscsictl add target iqn.2015-01.com.example:target,192.168.1.100
iscsictl login iqn.2015-01.com.example:target
.Ed
.Pp
Set the CHAP credentials for a target and log in to it automatically:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
iscsictl modify target-config iqn.2015-01.com.example:target \e
    -initiator-authentication CHAP -initiator-CHAP-name user \e
    -initiator-CHAP-secret -auto-login enable
iscsictl daemon install
.Ed
.Pp
See what was attached, then log out:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
iscsictl list luns
iscsictl logout iqn.2015-01.com.example:target
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr diskutil 8 ,
.Xr iscsid 8
